{
  "question": "Assess whether NAB's FY25 Business & Private Banking growth was value-accretive rather than growth bought at the expense of margin or credit quality. Reconcile lending, revenue and cash-earnings growth with the NIM, impairment-charge and sector/NPL disclosures, and identify what is broad-based versus single-name driven.",
  "bank": "NAB",
  "periods": [
    "FY25"
  ],
  "answer": "**Verdict: broadly value-accretive on returns, but the quality of the growth is mixed \u2014 margin gave ground and 2H25 asset-quality deterioration, while partly single-name, was concentrated in the very portfolio NAB is growing.**\n\n**Reconciliation (FY25 v FY24, cash basis, restated comparatives).** B&PB grew gross loans and acceptances 6.8% to $279.5bn (business lending +7.3% to $166.3bn; housing +5.8% to $109.3bn) and deposits +6.6% to $237.9bn. Yet net operating income rose only 2.1% to $8,531m (NII +2.8% to $7,499m) and cash earnings just 1.6% to $3,330m \u2014 so revenue grew at roughly a quarter of the pace of lending. The gap is the NIM: down 8bps to 3.01% on \"competitive pressures impacting both the business and housing lending portfolios, as well as higher deposit costs including mix impacts due to term deposit growth,\" partly offset by deposit/capital replicating portfolio earnings. Operating expenses rose 2.9%, lifting the cost-to-income ratio 30bps to 37.9%.\n\n**Margin vs credit quality.** The margin give-back was not redeemed by a falling impairment charge in absolute terms \u2014 it rose $6m (+1.1%) to $529m, and 2H25 jumped 23.2% HoH to $292m on \"higher business lending charges driven by higher levels of impairment, asset quality deterioration and volume growth.\" But relative to the larger book the charge-to-GLAs ratio actually fell 1bp to 0.19%, so credit cost did not erode value at the margin. Returns were flat, not accretive: cash earnings on average RWA slipped 1bp to 2.18% while RWAs grew 9.0% \u2014 faster than lending \u2014 \"mostly due to growth in business lending volumes, combined with deterioration in asset quality.\"\n\n**Broad-based vs single-name.** Group NPLs rose 18.2% to $12,094m, \"mainly driven by deterioration in the Business and Private Banking business lending portfolio\"; impaired assets rose 53.9% and impaired assets/GLAs 10bps to 0.49% for the division. However, the presentation isolates single-name risk: the B&PB business lending NPL ratio rose from 2.66% (Sep 24) to 3.22% (Sep 25), but \"excluding two large well secured Agri customers, underlying NPL ratio down 3bps to 3.01%\" (those two customers contribute 21bps), with \"stable to improving NPL outcomes in 2H25 for most sectors.\" So the ratio deterioration is substantially two well-secured Agri names; the growth itself (business lending +6.7%, deposits +6.6%) is broad-based.\n\n**Net:** volume growth is real and diversified and credit cost per unit of lending is stable, but flat RWA returns and an 8bps NIM decline mean the growth was not clearly value-accretive \u2014 more margin-neutral volume bought at stable, slightly deteriorating credit quality.\n\n**What the evidence does not settle:** whether individual pricing on new business lending was accretive (NAB discloses portfolio NIM only, no loan-level ROE), the identity/loss content of the two Agri customers, and the post-restatement comparability of FY24 NIM (portfolios were transferred between divisions).",
